# Line-ending policy.
#
# Two classes of file, deliberately treated differently:
#
#   design sources     normalised to LF in the repository AND in the working
#                      tree, so a checkout is byte-identical on every platform
#                      and a frozen-fixture digest means something.
#
#   production output  never touched. Gerbers, drills, the fabrication archive
#                      and the BOM/CPL that ship to a fabricator are hashed as
#                      exact raw bytes; normalising them after generation would
#                      invalidate the hashes recorded in the release manifest.
#
# The live board's approved waivers are bound to the SHA-256 of the RAW bytes
# of the native board and project files, so a change here unbinds them. That
# is a tripwire, not a prohibition: change it deliberately and re-approve the
# waivers against the new bytes, which is what the Linux migration did.

# Default: text, normalised to LF in the repo and on checkout.
*                    text=auto eol=lf

# --- KiCad design files: text, LF everywhere -------------------------------
#
# `eol=lf`, matching the default above, because the canonical environment is
# Linux and KiCad there writes LF.
#
# This used to be `eol=crlf`. That existed for exactly one reason: the two
# approved DRC waivers in board/manifest.live.json record
# approved_source_sha256 / approved_rules_sha256 over these files' RAW bytes,
# and had been reviewed on Windows against the CRLF form (51,958 CRLF pairs in
# the board). Keeping CRLF in the working tree kept those two digests valid.
#
# On Linux that pin was not merely redundant, it was broken. The clean-room
# release re-saves the board through `kicad-cli pcb drc --refill-zones
# --save-board`, and KiCad on Linux writes LF - so the board the release
# actually validates could never carry the CRLF digest the waivers demanded,
# and no release could ever pass. The two forms were proven byte-identical
# after LF normalisation; the whole difference was one \r per line.
#
# So the files are LF now and both waivers were re-approved against the LF
# bytes. Nothing else moved: pcbqa.canonical already computed its digest over
# LF, so the committed check reports under generated/release/reports/ and every
# source-closure digest were unaffected by the switch.
*.kicad_pcb          text eol=lf
*.kicad_sch          text eol=lf
*.kicad_pro          text eol=lf
*.kicad_prl          text eol=lf
*.kicad_sym          text eol=lf
*.kicad_mod          text eol=lf
*.kicad_dru          text eol=lf
*.kicad_wks          text eol=lf

# --- Project sources: explicit LF -----------------------------------------
fp-lib-table         text eol=lf
sym-lib-table        text eol=lf
*.py                 text eol=lf
*.md                 text eol=lf
*.json               text eol=lf
*.net                text eol=lf
*.csv                text eol=lf

# --- Production / fabrication output: exact bytes, never normalised -------
# Anything under a release directory is treated as binary so Git performs no
# EOL translation in either direction.
generated/release/** -text

# --- Frozen upstream evidence: exact bytes --------------------------------
# The SHA-256 of these response bodies is the evidence. They happen to contain
# no line endings at all today, so normalisation would be a no-op - but the
# point is that Git is never in a position to touch them.
fabrication/jlc_orientation/raw/** -text

*.gbr                binary
*.gbrjob             binary
*.drl                binary
*.zip                binary
*.pdf                binary
*.png                binary
*.jpg                binary
*.step               binary
*.stp                binary
*.wrl                binary
